WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.365 --> 00:00:02.821
- We think of vulnerability
as a dark emotion.

00:00:02.821 --> 00:00:05.098
We think of it as the core of

00:00:05.098 --> 00:00:07.370
fear and shame and grief

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.594
and disappointment, uncertainty.

00:00:09.594 --> 00:00:11.788
Things that we do not want to feel right.

00:00:11.788 --> 00:00:14.249
I call perfectionism the 2010 shield.

00:00:14.249 --> 00:00:16.437
We carry it around thinking
it's going to protect us

00:00:16.437 --> 00:00:17.426
from being hurt.
- Yeah.

00:00:17.426 --> 00:00:18.617
- But it protects us from being seen.

00:00:18.617 --> 00:00:20.343
But how many of you
were raised in families

00:00:20.343 --> 00:00:21.579
where you encouraged to get
curious about your emotions

00:00:21.579 --> 00:00:24.747
and talk about them
and explore them right?

00:00:24.747 --> 00:00:26.519
Versus how many of you
were raised in families

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:28.275
where you were taught, hey suck it up.

00:00:28.275 --> 00:00:29.559
- Yep.
- Push through

00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:30.773
and get it done.

00:00:30.773 --> 00:00:33.221
- Hello a Believe Nation,
my name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:33.221 --> 00:00:36.651
My one reason to believe
in entrepreneurs will solve

00:00:36.651 --> 00:00:38.513
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:40.484
So now open up your
journey today we're going

00:00:40.484 --> 00:00:42.639
to learn from best
selling author Brene Brown

00:00:42.639 --> 00:00:45.365
and my take on her top
10 rules to success.

00:00:45.365 --> 00:00:47.455
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:47.455 --> 00:00:49.871
and I'd love to know which
ones you guys like best.

00:00:49.871 --> 00:00:51.769
And as always as you're watching

00:00:51.769 --> 00:00:54.137
if you see something that
really resonates_ with you

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:55.828
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:55.828 --> 00:00:57.508
or quotes so other people can be inspired.

00:00:57.508 --> 00:00:58.951
And you can win a prize, as well.

00:00:58.951 --> 00:01:01.155
And also when you write it
down it's much more likely

00:01:01.155 --> 00:01:03.317
to stick for yourself, as well.

00:01:03.317 --> 00:01:04.150
Enjoy.

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:06.478
(airplane noise)

00:01:06.478 --> 00:01:07.311
(basketball bounce)

00:01:07.311 --> 00:01:09.644
(pop music)

00:01:17.552 --> 00:01:20.514
- And so my story is
that I am a researcher

00:01:20.514 --> 00:01:23.980
and I never thought I would
have a big public career.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:27.917
And so I did a TED talk
that went very viral.

00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:31.819
And in the wake of that
I was kind of everywhere

00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:35.927
for a couple of months on every CNN.com,

00:01:35.927 --> 00:01:37.905
NPR it was everywhere.

00:01:37.905 --> 00:01:40.153
And something it was
something I wasn't used to.

00:01:40.153 --> 00:01:42.716
And the marching orders

00:01:42.716 --> 00:01:44.699
from my therapist and my husband

00:01:44.699 --> 00:01:47.912
were, do not read the comments online.

00:01:47.912 --> 00:01:48.745
(laughing)

00:01:48.745 --> 00:01:50.752
So I read all the comments online.

00:01:50.752 --> 00:01:53.395
(laughing)

00:01:53.395 --> 00:01:55.898
It's, so one morning I woke up

00:01:55.898 --> 00:01:58.469
and there were two or
three new articles out

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:01.371
and I started reading the comments.

00:02:01.371 --> 00:02:03.531
And they were devastating.

00:02:03.531 --> 00:02:05.472
They weren't about my
work, they were about me,

00:02:05.472 --> 00:02:07.312
they were super personal.

00:02:07.312 --> 00:02:09.312
And they were the things

00:02:10.653 --> 00:02:12.320
that creative people

00:02:13.387 --> 00:02:15.523
play in their mind and then give up

00:02:15.523 --> 00:02:17.701
doing what they really want to do.

00:02:17.701 --> 00:02:21.462
Like if I asked every single one of you

00:02:21.462 --> 00:02:25.126
you would try, what
would you try if you knew

00:02:25.126 --> 00:02:28.218
people would never say this about you?

00:02:28.218 --> 00:02:31.112
What would that, what would this be?

00:02:31.112 --> 00:02:33.847
It would, those were the
comments that morning.

00:02:33.847 --> 00:02:35.746
Of course she embraces imperfection,

00:02:35.746 --> 00:02:36.860
what choice does she have?

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.250
Look at how she looks.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.686
I feel sorry for her kids.

00:02:41.686 --> 00:02:43.769
Less research more Botox.

00:02:45.603 --> 00:02:49.733
Just mean personal attacks,
the things that really

00:02:49.733 --> 00:02:52.033
up until that moment

00:02:52.033 --> 00:02:52.866
had

00:02:55.031 --> 00:02:56.954
inspired me to stay

00:02:56.954 --> 00:02:59.266
very small in my life and in my career

00:02:59.266 --> 00:03:02.159
just so I could avoid these things.

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:04.515
So that morning Steve the kids leave.

00:03:04.515 --> 00:03:05.432
I say home.

00:03:06.724 --> 00:03:09.232
I get on the couch and
I watch eight hours of

00:03:09.232 --> 00:03:10.425
Downton Abbey

00:03:10.425 --> 00:03:12.652
(laughing)

00:03:12.652 --> 00:03:14.767
And when it's over I don't want to turn

00:03:14.767 --> 00:03:16.005
off Downton Abbey

00:03:16.005 --> 00:03:18.360
because I then, because the
minute you turn off Downton

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:20.657
Abbey it's like soccer practice and dinner

00:03:20.657 --> 00:03:22.446
and back to the mean people.

00:03:22.446 --> 00:03:24.268
And maybe, should I get Botox?

00:03:24.268 --> 00:03:29.049
And maybe you know, maybe if
I stand still when I talk.

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:32.370
So I get my laptop and I do a search for

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.022
who was president in the United States

00:03:34.022 --> 00:03:35.873
during the Downton Abbey era.

00:03:35.873 --> 00:03:37.592
Have you ever done that
like you're numbing with

00:03:37.592 --> 00:03:39.202
TV or movie and so when it's over you

00:03:39.202 --> 00:03:41.173
just like stay in that space by like

00:03:41.173 --> 00:03:43.454
learning more about the actors and

00:03:43.454 --> 00:03:45.811
what's going on?
(laughing)

00:03:45.811 --> 00:03:48.303
I've been doing this long enough
to know this is like you're

00:03:48.303 --> 00:03:50.544
laughing with me, not at me.

00:03:50.544 --> 00:03:51.571
(laughing)

00:03:51.571 --> 00:03:54.379
So I put it in and Theodore
Roosevelt comes up and a

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:55.629
quote comes up.

00:03:56.949 --> 00:03:59.033
And I read it and this is what it says.

00:03:59.033 --> 00:04:01.604
It's a quote from a speech
that he gave in the early

00:04:01.604 --> 00:04:03.878
1900's of (mumbling)

00:04:03.878 --> 00:04:06.818
And a lot of people call it
the man in the arena speech.

00:04:06.818 --> 00:04:10.410
And this is the passage that
changed-- changes my life.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.201
It's not the critic who counts.

00:04:13.201 --> 00:04:14.858
It's not the man who points out

00:04:14.858 --> 00:04:16.562
how the strong man stumbles or where the

00:04:16.562 --> 00:04:18.733
doer of deeds could have done it better.

00:04:18.733 --> 00:04:22.364
The credit belongs to the person
who's actually in the arena

00:04:22.364 --> 00:04:26.119
whose face is marred with
blood and sweat and dust.

00:04:26.119 --> 00:04:29.854
Who, at the best in the
end knows the triumph

00:04:29.854 --> 00:04:31.376
of high achievement.

00:04:31.376 --> 00:04:34.350
And who at worst, if he failed

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.350
he fails daring greatly.

00:04:38.313 --> 00:04:40.960
So the moment that I read
that I closed my laptop

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:42.803
and this is what shifted in me.

00:04:42.803 --> 00:04:44.025
Three huge things.

00:04:44.025 --> 00:04:48.159
First, I spent the last 12
years studying vulnerability

00:04:48.159 --> 00:04:52.454
and that quote was everything
I know about vulnerability.

00:04:52.454 --> 00:04:53.733
It is not about winning

00:04:53.733 --> 00:04:55.637
it's not about losing.

00:04:55.637 --> 00:04:58.720
It's about showing up and being seen.

00:04:59.713 --> 00:05:03.296
The second thing, this
is who I want to be.

00:05:04.357 --> 00:05:06.015
I want to create.

00:05:06.015 --> 00:05:10.034
I want to make things
that didn't exist before.

00:05:10.034 --> 00:05:10.867
I touched them.

00:05:10.867 --> 00:05:13.353
I want to show up and be seen in my work

00:05:13.353 --> 00:05:14.466
and in my life.

00:05:14.466 --> 00:05:17.378
And if you're going to show up and be seen

00:05:17.378 --> 00:05:19.491
there is only one guarantee.

00:05:19.491 --> 00:05:23.071
And that is, you will get your ass kicked.

00:05:23.071 --> 00:05:24.346
That is the guarantee.

00:05:24.346 --> 00:05:25.744
That's the only certainty you have

00:05:25.744 --> 00:05:27.211
if you're going to go in the arena

00:05:27.211 --> 00:05:29.573
and spend any time in there whatsoever

00:05:29.573 --> 00:05:32.123
especially if you've committed to

00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:35.180
creating in your life you
will get your ass kicked.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:39.099
So you have to decide at that
moment I think for all of us

00:05:39.099 --> 00:05:41.585
if courage is a value that we hold

00:05:41.585 --> 00:05:43.441
this is a consequence.

00:05:43.441 --> 00:05:45.459
You can't avoid it.

00:05:45.459 --> 00:05:48.069
The third thing which really set me free

00:05:48.069 --> 00:05:49.738
and I think Steve my husband would argue

00:05:49.738 --> 00:05:52.701
has made me somewhat dangerous.

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:56.192
Is kind of a new
philosophy about criticism

00:05:56.192 --> 00:05:59.242
which is this, if you're not in the arena

00:05:59.242 --> 00:06:00.649
also getting your ass kicked

00:06:00.649 --> 00:06:03.814
I'm not interested in your feedback.

00:06:03.814 --> 00:06:06.369
(laughing and applause)

00:06:06.369 --> 00:06:07.624
- [Interviewer] what does it mean to be

00:06:07.624 --> 00:06:08.457
a wholehearted person?

00:06:08.457 --> 00:06:10.973
You have actually you know a list

00:06:10.973 --> 00:06:14.281
of ten different qualities
that wholehearted people

00:06:14.281 --> 00:06:15.541
have in common.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.494
And so cultivating authenticity.

00:06:18.494 --> 00:06:22.025
Letting go of what people
think that's the first one

00:06:22.025 --> 00:06:23.211
let's talk about that.

00:06:23.211 --> 00:06:25.219
- [Brene] It's so hard I
thought doing this research

00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:27.026
I thought going into it
you're authentic people

00:06:27.026 --> 00:06:28.262
and inauthentic people.

00:06:28.262 --> 00:06:31.118
I had I did not find any
evidence of that at all.

00:06:31.118 --> 00:06:33.885
What I found its
authenticity is a practice

00:06:33.885 --> 00:06:35.725
and you choose it every
day sometimes every

00:06:35.725 --> 00:06:37.557
hour of every day.

00:06:37.557 --> 00:06:39.242
And it's a practice.

00:06:39.242 --> 00:06:41.387
It's not I just like, I am authentic.

00:06:41.387 --> 00:06:43.120
It's that when you walk into a meeting

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.151
you have to make the choice,
am I going to show up

00:06:45.151 --> 00:06:45.984
and let myself be seen?

00:06:45.984 --> 00:06:48.322
Am I going to if I can raise my hand say,

00:06:48.322 --> 00:06:50.494
Wow you all look super
excited I don't know

00:06:50.494 --> 00:06:52.342
what in the hell you're talking about.

00:06:52.342 --> 00:06:53.597
(laughing)

00:06:53.597 --> 00:06:55.113
You know that's a choice.

00:06:55.113 --> 00:06:55.946
- Yes uh huh.

00:06:55.946 --> 00:06:56.794
- Right.

00:06:56.794 --> 00:06:59.581
And to be make that
authentic choice you got to

00:06:59.581 --> 00:07:00.414
let go of.

00:07:01.529 --> 00:07:03.767
- Of the faker faker ooh, I say.

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:05.329
Yeah, call it the faker ooh.

00:07:05.329 --> 00:07:07.362
But you know what I have found I mean

00:07:07.362 --> 00:07:09.154
I consider myself to
be an authentic person

00:07:09.154 --> 00:07:12.129
but when I am inauthentic
is when I've allowed myself

00:07:12.129 --> 00:07:14.848
to be around people who were not.

00:07:14.848 --> 00:07:16.954
And then I have to fake it

00:07:16.954 --> 00:07:18.320
to be with them.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:20.595
- [Brene] Oh for sure, It's contagious

00:07:20.595 --> 00:07:21.933
- [Oprah] Yes so they're faking it.

00:07:21.933 --> 00:07:22.766
- Yeah.

00:07:22.766 --> 00:07:23.789
- [Opah] And then and you
know you're in that situation

00:07:23.789 --> 00:07:25.656
when you do that uh huh
that kind of uh huh.

00:07:25.656 --> 00:07:28.219
You're laughing at
jokes that aren't funny.

00:07:28.219 --> 00:07:31.348
You're pretending to be
comfortable when you're not

00:07:31.348 --> 00:07:33.655
and lose your own authenticity.

00:07:33.655 --> 00:07:34.835
- Yeah, and I do it.

00:07:34.835 --> 00:07:37.442
One of the shocking findings of my work

00:07:37.442 --> 00:07:40.471
was the idea that the
most compassionate people

00:07:40.471 --> 00:07:43.266
I have interviewed over the last 13 years

00:07:43.266 --> 00:07:47.023
were also the absolutely most boundaried.

00:07:47.023 --> 00:07:49.056
- [Interviewer] The most boundaried?

00:07:49.056 --> 00:07:51.836
- I'll give you the definition of boundary

00:07:51.836 --> 00:07:54.030
that I use in the book boundary is simply

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:55.997
what's okay and what's not okay.

00:07:55.997 --> 00:07:59.796
What I think we do is
we don't set boundaries.

00:07:59.796 --> 00:08:01.713
We let people do things

00:08:02.932 --> 00:08:04.359
that are not okay

00:08:04.359 --> 00:08:06.433
or get away with behaviors
that are not okay.

00:08:06.433 --> 00:08:09.163
Then we're just resentful people.

00:08:09.163 --> 00:08:12.217
Me, I'd rather be loving and generous

00:08:12.217 --> 00:08:14.377
and very straightforward with what's okay

00:08:14.377 --> 00:08:15.433
and what's not okay.

00:08:15.433 --> 00:08:17.598
So my question is big B-I-G.

00:08:17.598 --> 00:08:19.863
What boundaries need to be in place

00:08:19.863 --> 00:08:22.181
for me to stay in my integrity

00:08:22.181 --> 00:08:26.014
and make the most generous
assumptions about you.

00:08:26.014 --> 00:08:28.574
The generosity can't
exist without boundaries

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:32.113
and we are not comfortable
setting boundaries.

00:08:32.113 --> 00:08:35.161
Because we care more about
what people will think

00:08:35.161 --> 00:08:37.242
and we don't want to disappoint anyone.

00:08:37.242 --> 00:08:38.873
We want everyone to like us

00:08:38.873 --> 00:08:41.206
and boundaries are not easy.

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:45.000
But I think they're the key to self love

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.262
and I think they're the
key to treating others

00:08:47.262 --> 00:08:49.733
with loving kindness.

00:08:49.733 --> 00:08:50.761
- [Interviewer] To sustain.

00:08:50.761 --> 00:08:51.594
- To sustain.

00:08:51.594 --> 00:08:54.296
Nothing is sustainable without boundaries.

00:08:54.296 --> 00:08:57.796
When I found you know 12 years of research

00:08:58.742 --> 00:09:01.988
11,000 pieces of data I did not interview

00:09:01.988 --> 00:09:05.129
in all that time a
person who would describe

00:09:05.129 --> 00:09:07.282
themselves as joyful.

00:09:07.282 --> 00:09:09.549
Or described their lives as joyous.

00:09:09.549 --> 00:09:12.887
Who did not actively practice gratitude.

00:09:12.887 --> 00:09:16.557
And for me it was very counterintuitive

00:09:16.557 --> 00:09:19.275
because I kind of went
into the research thinking

00:09:19.275 --> 00:09:22.288
that the relationship
between joy and gratitude

00:09:22.288 --> 00:09:26.514
was if you're joyful then
you should be grateful.

00:09:26.514 --> 00:09:28.225
But it wasn't that way at all

00:09:28.225 --> 00:09:30.789
it was really that practicing gratitude

00:09:30.789 --> 00:09:33.082
invites joy into our lives.

00:09:33.082 --> 00:09:35.461
And when I say practice I think this is

00:09:35.461 --> 00:09:37.512
this is the part that
really changed my life,

00:09:37.512 --> 00:09:40.010
it changed my family the
way we live every day.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:41.725
When I say practice gratitude

00:09:41.725 --> 00:09:44.405
I don't mean kind of like
the attitude of gratitude

00:09:44.405 --> 00:09:48.357
or feeling grateful I
mean practicing gratitude.

00:09:48.357 --> 00:09:50.673
These folks shared in common

00:09:50.673 --> 00:09:53.256
a tangible gratitude practice.

00:09:53.256 --> 00:09:55.928
They either kept gratitude journals.

00:09:55.928 --> 00:09:57.759
Some of them did
interesting things like at

00:09:57.759 --> 00:10:00.407
one, two, three, four like
at 12:30 for every day

00:10:00.407 --> 00:10:03.154
they said something out loud
that they were grateful for.

00:10:03.154 --> 00:10:04.890
They one of the things that

00:10:04.890 --> 00:10:07.047
we do like we say grace at dinner.

00:10:07.047 --> 00:10:08.566
And so now after grace we go around and

00:10:08.566 --> 00:10:11.047
everyone in my family says
something they're grateful for.

00:10:11.047 --> 00:10:12.528
I mean including what's
interesting is when we first

00:10:12.528 --> 00:10:15.003
started I have a first grader at first

00:10:15.003 --> 00:10:17.708
grader son Charlie and
eighth grade daughter Ellen

00:10:17.708 --> 00:10:19.590
and at first I thought and
we've been doing it for a couple

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.972
years, now like they're like, oh God mom.

00:10:21.972 --> 00:10:23.822
And if there was a little like this is

00:10:23.822 --> 00:10:26.524
are you experimenting on us? (laughs)

00:10:26.524 --> 00:10:29.728
A little bit of that but
now what's interesting

00:10:29.728 --> 00:10:31.683
even after we did it for
like a couple of weeks

00:10:31.683 --> 00:10:33.989
that on those crazy busy
nights where we're trying to

00:10:33.989 --> 00:10:37.985
like get to soccer and piano and homework.

00:10:37.985 --> 00:10:39.608
And Steve and I are just like

00:10:39.608 --> 00:10:40.895
we say a quick prayer we start eating

00:10:40.895 --> 00:10:42.570
my kids are like whoa,

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:44.320
what are you grateful for?

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:47.095
And it's been extraordinary because

00:10:47.095 --> 00:10:48.678
not only absolutely

00:10:50.523 --> 00:10:53.089
does it invite more joy into our house.

00:10:53.089 --> 00:10:56.162
It also is such a soulful window into

00:10:56.162 --> 00:10:57.976
what's going on in my kids lives.

00:10:57.976 --> 00:11:00.996
You know so there's some days
my eighth grader will be like,

00:11:00.996 --> 00:11:03.336
I'm joyful that there's
a huge thick wall between

00:11:03.336 --> 00:11:04.720
my room and my brother's room you know

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:07.763
something just very you know honest.

00:11:07.763 --> 00:11:10.154
But there other days she'll say you know

00:11:10.154 --> 00:11:13.537
she had a friend his mother recently died

00:11:13.537 --> 00:11:17.163
and she said you know for
a month she would say,

00:11:17.163 --> 00:11:19.496
I'm just so grateful that you
all are healthy right now.

00:11:19.496 --> 00:11:22.914
You know and so not only
did it make us all more

00:11:22.914 --> 00:11:24.760
aware of what we had

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.301
and more willing to
slow down and really be

00:11:27.301 --> 00:11:29.738
thankful for the joyful moments we had.

00:11:29.738 --> 00:11:32.058
But it let me know where she
was emotionally in her life.

00:11:32.058 --> 00:11:34.239
You know my son is always, you know,

00:11:34.239 --> 00:11:36.616
I'm grateful for bugs,
I'm grateful for frogs.

00:11:36.616 --> 00:11:38.678
But sometimes he'll say you know,

00:11:38.678 --> 00:11:40.640
I'm grateful that you picked me up early.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:42.902
Or you know, I'm grateful that I finally

00:11:42.902 --> 00:11:44.704
understand adjectives you know.

00:11:44.704 --> 00:11:47.121
So it's there's a great quote

00:11:50.028 --> 00:11:52.753
that says it's not granted it's not joy

00:11:52.753 --> 00:11:55.549
that makes us grateful it's
gratitude it makes us joyful.

00:11:55.549 --> 00:11:59.775
And it's by a Jesuit
brother a Jesuit priest.

00:11:59.775 --> 00:12:01.192
And I guess I was

00:12:02.325 --> 00:12:05.795
just amazed to find that
bubble up so strongly

00:12:05.795 --> 00:12:06.903
in the research.

00:12:06.903 --> 00:12:08.200
It's life-changing.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:12.787
We think about vulnerability
as a dark emotion.

00:12:12.787 --> 00:12:15.526
You know there are a lot of
people who talk about light

00:12:15.526 --> 00:12:17.631
or you know positive
emotions, negative emotions,

00:12:17.631 --> 00:12:19.578
dark emotions, light emotions.

00:12:19.578 --> 00:12:22.156
We think of vulnerability
as a dark emotion.

00:12:22.156 --> 00:12:25.147
We think of it as the
core of fear and shame

00:12:25.147 --> 00:12:28.991
and grief and disappointment, uncertainty.

00:12:28.991 --> 00:12:31.508
Things that we do not want to feel right.

00:12:31.508 --> 00:12:33.330
Things that I don't want to be vulnerable

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:36.742
because that means I'm afraid,
that means I'm uncertain.

00:12:36.742 --> 00:12:40.017
That means I'm at risk, I'm exposed.

00:12:40.017 --> 00:12:41.100
I'm in grief.

00:12:42.566 --> 00:12:45.573
So what we do is we armor up

00:12:45.573 --> 00:12:47.831
and we say I do not want
to slip into these dark

00:12:47.831 --> 00:12:51.731
emotions I will not let
myself be vulnerable.

00:12:51.731 --> 00:12:52.564
But here's

00:12:54.501 --> 00:12:56.960
what I learn from the research

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.410
and certainly put into
motion in my own life

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:00.243
that was

00:13:01.657 --> 00:13:04.202
the most life-changing
is that vulnerability is

00:13:04.202 --> 00:13:07.290
the center of difficult emotion.

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:09.623
But it's also the birthplace

00:13:10.604 --> 00:13:12.658
of every positive emotion

00:13:12.658 --> 00:13:14.291
that we need in our lives.

00:13:14.291 --> 00:13:16.791
Love, belonging, joy, empathy.

00:13:18.608 --> 00:13:20.601
How many of you would agree
that we're in a serious

00:13:20.601 --> 00:13:23.896
empathy deficit in our culture today?

00:13:23.896 --> 00:13:25.146
Totally, right?

00:13:26.496 --> 00:13:28.913
No vulnerability, no empathy.

00:13:29.756 --> 00:13:33.294
And a culture where people
are afraid to be vulnerable

00:13:33.294 --> 00:13:35.211
you can't have empathy.

00:13:36.754 --> 00:13:39.728
Here empathy is not a default response.

00:13:39.728 --> 00:13:42.618
If you share something
with me that's difficult

00:13:42.618 --> 00:13:45.668
in order for me to be truly empathic

00:13:45.668 --> 00:13:49.477
I have to step into what you're feeling.

00:13:49.477 --> 00:13:51.676
And that's vulnerable.

00:13:51.676 --> 00:13:55.528
So there can be no empathy
without vulnerability.

00:13:55.528 --> 00:13:58.080
Why do you think in that
example that I used while

00:13:58.080 --> 00:13:59.997
ago daughter comes home

00:14:00.979 --> 00:14:03.525
and says you know tears.

00:14:03.525 --> 00:14:05.966
No one sat with me at
lunch today and made fun

00:14:05.966 --> 00:14:07.682
of what I was wearing.

00:14:07.682 --> 00:14:09.958
So-and-so won't talk to me.

00:14:09.958 --> 00:14:12.583
They poured my books out of my locker.

00:14:12.583 --> 00:14:15.144
And the response back is,

00:14:15.144 --> 00:14:17.498
I told you I bought you
all those cute jeans.

00:14:17.498 --> 00:14:19.115
Why aren't you wearing those jeans

00:14:19.115 --> 00:14:22.158
and pulling your hair back.

00:14:22.158 --> 00:14:24.681
Is that an empathic response?

00:14:24.681 --> 00:14:27.143
No, it's a shaming response.

00:14:27.143 --> 00:14:31.272
Could that shaming
response be, could a mother

00:14:31.272 --> 00:14:34.629
who absolutely adores her child

00:14:34.629 --> 00:14:37.203
respond with that shaming response?

00:14:37.203 --> 00:14:38.950
Please say yes, don't kid yourself.

00:14:38.950 --> 00:14:40.337
I mean come on.

00:14:40.337 --> 00:14:42.138
If you've got a if you're
a parent sitting in here

00:14:42.138 --> 00:14:45.324
then you sure as hell know
the answer to that is yes.

00:14:45.324 --> 00:14:47.741
But why, why did that happen?

00:14:48.871 --> 00:14:51.043
What where was the
access to vulnerability?

00:14:51.043 --> 00:14:52.999
Where was, I mean, where was empathy?

00:14:52.999 --> 00:14:54.999
You can't access empathy

00:14:55.922 --> 00:14:57.918
if you're not willing to be vulnerable.

00:14:57.918 --> 00:15:00.146
So if my daughter comes
home and tells that to me,

00:15:00.146 --> 00:15:01.854
guess what I have to do?

00:15:01.854 --> 00:15:05.008
I have to reactivate that sweaty palmed

00:15:05.008 --> 00:15:07.925
seventh grader who lives inside me.

00:15:10.028 --> 00:15:12.028
And I have to go, oh God

00:15:13.197 --> 00:15:14.447
that's so hard.

00:15:16.643 --> 00:15:17.726
I'm so sorry.

00:15:19.086 --> 00:15:20.579
That's happened to me.

00:15:20.579 --> 00:15:22.641
That's happened to me when
I was in middle school

00:15:22.641 --> 00:15:25.474
and it's happened to me last week.

00:15:27.095 --> 00:15:28.762
Let's talk about it.

00:15:30.326 --> 00:15:33.209
But you can't get there
without vulnerability.

00:15:33.209 --> 00:15:35.126
You can't fake empathy.

00:15:37.445 --> 00:15:41.112
Innovation creativity
born of vulnerability.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:45.811
This is my favorite part I talked about

00:15:45.811 --> 00:15:48.438
this admit I did another
TED talk this year

00:15:48.438 --> 00:15:50.671
at Long Beach and I told the story

00:15:50.671 --> 00:15:54.373
that during 2011 and even this year

00:15:54.373 --> 00:15:57.632
after the big TEDX Houston talk went viral

00:15:57.632 --> 00:16:01.067
the big calls came from
Fortune 500 companies.

00:16:01.067 --> 00:16:03.627
Oh my God we loved your
TED talk, it was great.

00:16:03.627 --> 00:16:05.878
Please come talk to all
of our senior leaders.

00:16:05.878 --> 00:16:08.277
And it's like okay.

00:16:08.277 --> 00:16:10.037
What do you want to talk about?

00:16:10.037 --> 00:16:12.530
Like we don't care just
come and talk to us.

00:16:12.530 --> 00:16:16.049
Just if you could ixnay the
shame and vulnerability talk.

00:16:16.049 --> 00:16:17.627
(laughing)

00:16:17.627 --> 00:16:19.800
Every single conversation

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.284
barring maybe 10%.

00:16:22.284 --> 00:16:23.850
I was like, well what do you mean?

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:26.175
Well we let you know your you're funny

00:16:26.175 --> 00:16:27.312
you have this great research.

00:16:27.312 --> 00:16:29.923
I think there's a real
fit with what we do.

00:16:29.923 --> 00:16:32.468
We just we don't really
do you know that kind of

00:16:32.468 --> 00:16:34.622
stuff around here so you could not

00:16:34.622 --> 00:16:35.974
mention vulnerability and shame.

00:16:35.974 --> 00:16:37.559
(laughing)

00:16:37.559 --> 00:16:40.565
So just for fun for
grins I would say, okay,

00:16:40.565 --> 00:16:42.854
so what would you like me to talk about?

00:16:42.854 --> 00:16:45.097
You know, fourth-quarter earnings

00:16:45.097 --> 00:16:47.485
I frickin' don't even
balance my checkbook.

00:16:47.485 --> 00:16:48.318
(laughing)

00:16:48.318 --> 00:16:50.142
Like I'm not going to talk about that.

00:16:50.142 --> 00:16:53.487
So what do we what do
you want me talk about?

00:16:53.487 --> 00:16:57.404
Well, the big issue,
creativity and innovation.

00:16:58.846 --> 00:16:59.695
Mmm.

00:16:59.695 --> 00:17:02.272
And change, we're going
through a lot of change.

00:17:02.272 --> 00:17:05.481
It's like okay so vulnerability

00:17:05.481 --> 00:17:08.214
is the birthplace of
creativity innovation change.

00:17:08.214 --> 00:17:09.763
And the reason that crisis is happening is

00:17:09.763 --> 00:17:13.479
because you're not talking
about vulnerability.

00:17:13.479 --> 00:17:17.937
Imagine creativity innovation
without vulnerability.

00:17:17.937 --> 00:17:19.104
I'm asking you

00:17:20.053 --> 00:17:21.553
for a work product

00:17:22.555 --> 00:17:24.503
that has never been made before.

00:17:24.503 --> 00:17:27.406
That's completely innovative
I need you to be creative and

00:17:27.406 --> 00:17:29.570
I need a percent of people
who are going to happen

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:31.533
when I think it's stupid
and not understand it.

00:17:31.533 --> 00:17:33.544
No vulnerability there.

00:17:33.544 --> 00:17:36.954
Perfectionism what
emerged for me in the data

00:17:36.954 --> 00:17:40.109
was that perfectionism is not
about striving for excellence

00:17:40.109 --> 00:17:42.742
or healthy striving which I'm for.

00:17:42.742 --> 00:17:43.857
- [Oprah] Yeah.

00:17:43.857 --> 00:17:46.265
- It's a cognitive behavioral process

00:17:46.265 --> 00:17:48.718
a way of thinking and
feeling that says this.

00:17:48.718 --> 00:17:51.958
If I look perfect do
it perfect work perfect

00:17:51.958 --> 00:17:54.256
and live perfect I can avoid or minimize

00:17:54.256 --> 00:17:57.253
shame, blame and judgment.

00:17:57.253 --> 00:17:59.198
- [Oprah] You know what I
thought when I was reading this

00:17:59.198 --> 00:18:00.948
I had another aha aha

00:18:02.085 --> 00:18:03.137
(high five)

00:18:03.137 --> 00:18:04.137
- To people.

00:18:05.147 --> 00:18:07.493
- This was my other aha

00:18:07.493 --> 00:18:09.677
that perfectionism I
never gotten this before

00:18:09.677 --> 00:18:12.248
that perfectionism is the ultimate fear.

00:18:12.248 --> 00:18:14.275
That the people who are walking around

00:18:14.275 --> 00:18:16.862
as perfectionists who have everything so.

00:18:16.862 --> 00:18:17.769
- Yeah.

00:18:17.769 --> 00:18:19.561
- [Oprah] That they are ultimately afraid

00:18:19.561 --> 00:18:21.292
that the world is going to see them for

00:18:21.292 --> 00:18:23.684
who they really are and
they won't measure up.

00:18:23.684 --> 00:18:25.302
- There's no question
that's that's what it

00:18:25.302 --> 00:18:27.167
that's right right that's
exactly what it is.

00:18:27.167 --> 00:18:28.436
It's fear.

00:18:28.436 --> 00:18:30.138
- [Oprah] Because it's
very different than trying

00:18:30.138 --> 00:18:32.218
to be excellent and working
hard and doing your best.

00:18:32.218 --> 00:18:33.397
- [Brene] Yeah yeah

00:18:33.397 --> 00:18:36.453
and so I call perfectionism
the 20-ton shield.

00:18:36.453 --> 00:18:38.494
We carry it around thinking
it is going to protect us

00:18:38.494 --> 00:18:39.528
from being hurt.
- Yeah.

00:18:39.528 --> 00:18:40.999
- But it protects us from being seen.

00:18:40.999 --> 00:18:43.285
Let me tell you this real
story this just happened.

00:18:43.285 --> 00:18:45.346
I spoke at HubSpot last week.

00:18:45.346 --> 00:18:47.896
Okay 13,000 people in this Lawson

00:18:47.896 --> 00:18:49.287
convention center.

00:18:49.287 --> 00:18:50.980
It's like 10 minutes before I go on

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:52.611
and I'm making that I look at Twitter

00:18:52.611 --> 00:18:56.719
and I'm this person sends
this tweet out that says,

00:18:56.719 --> 00:18:57.778
why is Brene at HubSpot?

00:18:57.778 --> 00:19:00.011
Why is a@BrenéBrown at HubSpot 2015?

00:19:00.011 --> 00:19:02.369
And he just tweeted like love Seth Godin,

00:19:02.369 --> 00:19:03.931
love Amy Schumer all these people.

00:19:03.931 --> 00:19:07.499
But why is Brene Brown,
and I'm like oh my God

00:19:07.499 --> 00:19:09.938
why am I at HubSpot, what am I doing here?

00:19:09.938 --> 00:19:12.530
And then I'm like I start sweating and

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:13.952
it's like any with the Convention Center

00:19:13.952 --> 00:19:16.191
is like because you
ladies you know I'm like

00:19:16.191 --> 00:19:18.298
oh my God what am I doing
on the opening keynote.

00:19:18.298 --> 00:19:22.562
I'm like and then he tweets it
again and I'm like oh my God.

00:19:22.562 --> 00:19:24.161
And so these are like marketing people

00:19:24.161 --> 00:19:26.960
I don't know anything so I
start Googling in my phone

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.019
marketing terms 2015.
(laughing)

00:19:29.019 --> 00:19:31.274
And great the first time that comes

00:19:31.274 --> 00:19:33.020
is incentivize, I'm like
I've got to work that

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:35.746
I've got to work the word
incentive into this this keynote.

00:19:35.746 --> 00:19:37.937
I'm like what does that
mean exactly, I don't know.

00:19:37.937 --> 00:19:40.372
But I'm going to say
we're going to incentivize

00:19:40.372 --> 00:19:41.965
I'm like what is happening

00:19:41.965 --> 00:19:44.517
and I have a total crisis of confidence.

00:19:44.517 --> 00:19:47.243
Like because you know
shame drives two tapes

00:19:47.243 --> 00:19:49.021
not good enough and who
do you think you are.

00:19:49.021 --> 00:19:51.028
And like who am I, right?

00:19:51.028 --> 00:19:52.361
That's terrible.

00:19:53.471 --> 00:19:57.360
And so then all of a
sudden I'm like who is it

00:19:57.360 --> 00:19:59.488
because what happens when
you get backed into a corner

00:19:59.488 --> 00:20:01.251
my brain is making up the story.

00:20:01.251 --> 00:20:03.011
You don't belong here,
I'm like that story's

00:20:03.011 --> 00:20:05.164
not going to work because
I'm going on in five seconds.

00:20:05.164 --> 00:20:07.227
(laughing)
I'm like so I'm like okay

00:20:07.227 --> 00:20:09.861
the best-case scenario attack.

00:20:09.861 --> 00:20:11.639
Attack, attack this guy.

00:20:11.639 --> 00:20:14.065
Maybe attack them from the stage.

00:20:14.065 --> 00:20:16.154
Maybe use that as my opening.

00:20:16.154 --> 00:20:18.071
Maybe say like John Doe

00:20:19.189 --> 00:20:21.269
asked what I'm doing
here, well let me tell you

00:20:21.269 --> 00:20:22.116
all, you know.

00:20:22.116 --> 00:20:25.652
And then I click on the tweet
to figure out what his name is

00:20:25.652 --> 00:20:27.487
and I accidentally hit
the link in his tweet

00:20:27.487 --> 00:20:29.632
and it goes to this page that says,

00:20:29.632 --> 00:20:31.067
What is Brené Brown doing at HubSpot?

00:20:31.067 --> 00:20:32.918
She's talking about vulnerability

00:20:32.918 --> 00:20:34.360
and that's so important.

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:36.317
Here's her TED talk.
(laughing)

00:20:36.317 --> 00:20:37.299
Here are her books.

00:20:37.299 --> 00:20:39.579
But could you imagine if
I would have got out there

00:20:39.579 --> 00:20:41.683
and been like a great fear.

00:20:41.683 --> 00:20:42.683
Yes totally.

00:20:43.616 --> 00:20:45.983
No and that's a true
story it just happened

00:20:45.983 --> 00:20:48.170
because what happens is
when something hard happens

00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:51.056
and were captured by something difficult

00:20:51.056 --> 00:20:53.737
our emotions get the first crack at making

00:20:53.737 --> 00:20:54.570
sense of something.

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:59.232
A bad look, a hard phone
call, a disagreement at work

00:20:59.232 --> 00:21:02.115
we think that we're rational
beings we think that cognition

00:21:02.115 --> 00:21:03.706
is going to carry us through
and make sense of it.

00:21:03.706 --> 00:21:06.661
But it doesn't you know emotions driving

00:21:06.661 --> 00:21:08.319
thought and behavior are not even in the

00:21:08.319 --> 00:21:09.605
front seat riding shotgun.

00:21:09.605 --> 00:21:11.140
They're not even the back seat.

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:13.860
Thought and behavior
and the trunk going hey

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:14.949
and emotions driving.

00:21:14.949 --> 00:21:17.351
So the first thing we
do is we tell ourselves

00:21:17.351 --> 00:21:21.242
a story that reduces
ambiguity about what happened.

00:21:21.242 --> 00:21:23.225
So, oh I'm not supposed to be here,

00:21:23.225 --> 00:21:24.705
I'm not good enough to be here.

00:21:24.705 --> 00:21:27.940
Men and women who have
the greatest capacity

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:31.962
for rising strong in the
moment something happens.

00:21:31.962 --> 00:21:34.508
They hack into that
neurobiological process

00:21:34.508 --> 00:21:36.141
of making up a story.

00:21:36.141 --> 00:21:39.177
They stop and say, wait a minute

00:21:39.177 --> 00:21:41.677
what's actually going on here?

00:21:42.644 --> 00:21:45.504
What is what am I feeling?

00:21:45.504 --> 00:21:46.849
What do I know for sure?

00:21:46.849 --> 00:21:48.991
Because what is a story?

00:21:48.991 --> 00:21:50.885
You know there's a name
and research for a story

00:21:50.885 --> 00:21:53.336
that has one or two limited data points.

00:21:53.336 --> 00:21:55.814
And we fill in the rest with fear.

00:21:55.814 --> 00:21:58.158
- Wow.
- It's called a conspiracy.

00:21:58.158 --> 00:21:59.121
(laughing)

00:21:59.121 --> 00:22:02.069
A conspiracy is a story
with limited data points.

00:22:02.069 --> 00:22:04.678
So here's what I know I
know. guy sent a tweet.

00:22:04.678 --> 00:22:06.355
I know I'm getting ready to talk

00:22:06.355 --> 00:22:07.465
that's all I know.

00:22:07.465 --> 00:22:10.134
So now that I'm ready to ruin his career

00:22:10.134 --> 00:22:12.114
and use him as a whipping person you know

00:22:12.114 --> 00:22:14.091
as I talk, what is going on?

00:22:14.091 --> 00:22:16.943
This first step of rising
strong is recognize you've

00:22:16.943 --> 00:22:20.010
been snagged by emotion and get
curious about it, that's it.

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:21.978
But how many of you
were raised in families

00:22:21.978 --> 00:22:23.391
where you encouraged to get
curious about your emotions

00:22:23.391 --> 00:22:26.057
and talk about them
and explore them right?

00:22:26.057 --> 00:22:26.890
(laughing)

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:28.998
Versus how many of you
were raised in families

00:22:28.998 --> 00:22:30.587
where you were taught, hey suck it up.

00:22:30.587 --> 00:22:31.420
- Yep.
- Push through

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:32.253
and get it done.

00:22:32.253 --> 00:22:35.273
So the first thing is really
reckoning with emotion.

00:22:35.273 --> 00:22:37.073
What am I feeling?

00:22:37.073 --> 00:22:39.888
And and what do I need to know more about?

00:22:39.888 --> 00:22:42.479
That is a huge, so that
thing that you say like

00:22:42.479 --> 00:22:44.686
does something that someone
knows something that I don't

00:22:44.686 --> 00:22:46.627
know, do they have
information I don't have?

00:22:46.627 --> 00:22:48.135
That's a huge part of the reckoning

00:22:48.135 --> 00:22:49.342
we just don't do it.

00:22:49.342 --> 00:22:50.983
So in that minute in the backstage

00:22:50.983 --> 00:22:53.229
when I was hopping on my
phone I could have just said,

00:22:53.229 --> 00:22:56.956
whoa Brene, your like heart is racing

00:22:56.956 --> 00:22:59.141
your where your teeth are clenched

00:22:59.141 --> 00:23:00.879
and you're going in for the kill here.

00:23:00.879 --> 00:23:03.212
What do you know about this?

00:23:04.712 --> 00:23:05.545
- Yeah.

00:23:05.545 --> 00:23:06.960
- Nothing, you know nothing.

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:08.385
And what if you do everything
who cares who gives a shit?

00:23:08.385 --> 00:23:09.218
- Yeah.

00:23:09.218 --> 00:23:11.093
- You know there are 13,000 people here

00:23:11.093 --> 00:23:12.846
you're going to spend an
hour targeting one guy

00:23:12.846 --> 00:23:13.679
you don't know?

00:23:13.679 --> 00:23:16.295
Shame is far more likely to
cause destructive behaviors

00:23:16.295 --> 00:23:18.663
than it is to cure it.

00:23:18.663 --> 00:23:21.099
What we know is that
shame is highly correlated

00:23:21.099 --> 00:23:24.874
with addiction, violence, aggression,

00:23:24.874 --> 00:23:25.707
bullying.

00:23:26.992 --> 00:23:29.127
It's not helpful to be ashamed.

00:23:29.127 --> 00:23:32.237
For this reason I think
this is really a core thing

00:23:32.237 --> 00:23:34.499
that we need to get our
heads and hearts around

00:23:34.499 --> 00:23:35.332
as a culture.

00:23:35.332 --> 00:23:38.090
Shame corrodes the part
of us that believes

00:23:38.090 --> 00:23:39.257
we can change.

00:23:40.312 --> 00:23:43.272
So to chain someone into changing

00:23:43.272 --> 00:23:46.935
is like saying to them you
are horrible and worthless

00:23:46.935 --> 00:23:48.870
and you're not capable of change.

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:49.703
Get better.

00:23:49.703 --> 00:23:52.142
I have interviewed over
600 men and women now

00:23:52.142 --> 00:23:54.179
across the country about shame

00:23:54.179 --> 00:23:57.057
and across the interviews men and women

00:23:57.057 --> 00:23:59.571
with high levels of resilience to shame

00:23:59.571 --> 00:24:02.072
share four things in common.

00:24:02.072 --> 00:24:04.773
The first is they physically recognize

00:24:04.773 --> 00:24:07.728
when they're in shame and
they know what triggered it.

00:24:07.728 --> 00:24:10.670
So let's say something happens
at work I make a mistake

00:24:10.670 --> 00:24:14.534
or something happens and
that warm wash comes over me.

00:24:14.534 --> 00:24:16.787
You know that feeling yes yeah.

00:24:16.787 --> 00:24:17.957
We all know it.
(laughs)

00:24:17.957 --> 00:24:20.345
If I can physically say

00:24:20.345 --> 00:24:23.568
oh my Go, this is shame, I'm in shame

00:24:23.568 --> 00:24:25.772
it's very helpful because we are not fit

00:24:25.772 --> 00:24:27.933
for human consumption when we're in shame.

00:24:27.933 --> 00:24:30.849
We need to pull away and get re-grounded,

00:24:30.849 --> 00:24:34.361
take some deep breaths and
figure out how to respond.

00:24:34.361 --> 00:24:36.793
One of the things that
makes aim worse is normally

00:24:36.793 --> 00:24:39.210
when we're in it, we act out.

00:24:40.097 --> 00:24:42.566
For example, the mother that I interviewed

00:24:42.566 --> 00:24:45.418
she fills her car up, she
goes into the gas station.

00:24:45.418 --> 00:24:46.507
She goes to pay.

00:24:46.507 --> 00:24:47.722
Her credit card's declined.

00:24:47.722 --> 00:24:50.176
The man shames her in
front of the other patrons

00:24:50.176 --> 00:24:52.615
makes her leave her I think
her wedding band and her wallet

00:24:52.615 --> 00:24:54.521
and leave with just an ATM card.

00:24:54.521 --> 00:24:56.673
So she's filled with
shame, she gets in the car

00:24:56.673 --> 00:24:58.282
she slams the door.

00:24:58.282 --> 00:25:00.237
When she slammed the door it
wakes up her three-year-old

00:25:00.237 --> 00:25:03.208
toddler who's in the backseat
who starts screaming.

00:25:03.208 --> 00:25:04.629
What happens next?

00:25:04.629 --> 00:25:07.227
She starts screaming
back, "Shut up, shut up."

00:25:07.227 --> 00:25:08.360
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:10.720
- So now she's in the shame
of having this credit card

00:25:10.720 --> 00:25:12.946
declined, being belittled
in front of people.

00:25:12.946 --> 00:25:15.055
And now having to deal with you know the

00:25:15.055 --> 00:25:16.392
struggle of, I'm a horrible mom

00:25:16.392 --> 00:25:18.107
and why am I taking this out of my child?

00:25:18.107 --> 00:25:20.619
So men and women with
high levels of resilience

00:25:20.619 --> 00:25:22.918
recognize physically when they're in shame

00:25:22.918 --> 00:25:24.383
and what triggered it.

00:25:24.383 --> 00:25:25.455
- Okay.
- Then they practice

00:25:25.455 --> 00:25:27.541
critical awareness.

00:25:27.541 --> 00:25:30.878
How realistic are the
expectations and messages

00:25:30.878 --> 00:25:34.499
that we put on ourselves
or that others put on us

00:25:34.499 --> 00:25:36.732
that tell us who we're supposed to be.

00:25:36.732 --> 00:25:38.776
The third thing is men and
women with high levels of

00:25:38.776 --> 00:25:40.563
resilience reach out.

00:25:40.563 --> 00:25:42.159
We've got to share our story.

00:25:42.159 --> 00:25:43.844
- [Interviewer] So they talk about it.

00:25:43.844 --> 00:25:45.115
- [Brene] They talk about it.

00:25:45.115 --> 00:25:47.410
You know we breathe through
it we recognize we're in it

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:49.394
and then we call and
say, hey Patricia it's me

00:25:49.394 --> 00:25:51.466
you're not going to believe what happened.

00:25:51.466 --> 00:25:52.327
Can you listen?

00:25:52.327 --> 00:25:53.372
It's hard.

00:25:53.372 --> 00:25:55.369
And then we speak shame you know

00:25:55.369 --> 00:25:58.105
it's I try to explain
to people the importance

00:25:58.105 --> 00:25:59.712
of using the word shame.

00:25:59.712 --> 00:26:02.296
And this is not just laypeople

00:26:02.296 --> 00:26:04.293
this is mental health professionals.

00:26:04.293 --> 00:26:05.268
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:26:05.268 --> 00:26:06.680
- You know we've got to use the word.

00:26:06.680 --> 00:26:08.658
It's different if I were
to call you and say,

00:26:08.658 --> 00:26:10.347
Oh Patricia Brene is so embarrassed

00:26:10.347 --> 00:26:11.730
that would be one thing right?

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:13.504
But if I were to call you and say,

00:26:13.504 --> 00:26:16.161
Patricia, it's Brene I'm really
struggling with some shame

00:26:16.161 --> 00:26:17.335
that's different.

00:26:17.335 --> 00:26:18.753
- [Interviewer] It's a
different conversation.

00:26:18.753 --> 00:26:19.586
- Right.

00:26:19.586 --> 00:26:21.637
Shame cannot hold on when we name it.

00:26:21.637 --> 00:26:22.470
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:24.148
- So it's not just you know
it's an academic pet peeve

00:26:24.148 --> 00:26:28.798
I want everybody to get
the semantics correct.

00:26:28.798 --> 00:26:30.189
Men and women with high
levels of shame resilience

00:26:30.189 --> 00:26:31.258
use the word and talk about the emotion.

00:26:31.258 --> 00:26:34.758
I think there's like a thin film of terror

00:26:35.807 --> 00:26:36.706
wrapped around us.

00:26:36.706 --> 00:26:38.238
And so if it's not

00:26:38.238 --> 00:26:41.256
I'm not safe enough,
I'm not secure enough.

00:26:41.256 --> 00:26:43.374
It's, I'm not liked enough,
I'm not promoted enough,

00:26:43.374 --> 00:26:44.898
I'm not loved enough.

00:26:44.898 --> 00:26:46.322
- [Oprah] I don't have enough.

00:26:46.322 --> 00:26:47.666
- I don't have enough.

00:26:47.666 --> 00:26:49.153
- I am not enough.

00:26:49.153 --> 00:26:50.509
- At the very bottom.
- [Oprah] Yeah.

00:26:50.509 --> 00:26:51.786
- That's not enough.
- [Oprah] Yeah.

00:26:51.786 --> 00:26:55.089
- Well that's big beg beg beg beg.

00:26:55.089 --> 00:26:56.671
Somebody ring some bells.

00:26:56.671 --> 00:26:58.694
We need some bells to ring on this show.

00:26:58.694 --> 00:27:01.349
- [Brene] Well that's it and
so guess what the number one

00:27:01.349 --> 00:27:04.182
casualty is of a scarcity culture.

00:27:05.687 --> 00:27:08.005
- [Oprah] What?
- [Brene] Vulnerability.

00:27:08.005 --> 00:27:10.102
- [Oprah] We shut down.
- [Brene] We shut down.

00:27:10.102 --> 00:27:12.086
- Because I'm not going to let you know.

00:27:12.086 --> 00:27:13.092
- [Brene] No.
- Because I don't

00:27:13.092 --> 00:27:16.164
I'm not all together.
- [Brene] Right.

00:27:16.164 --> 00:27:18.022
- Yeah.
- And the thing is

00:27:18.022 --> 00:27:19.832
that people like oh well we
lose a little vulnerability

00:27:19.832 --> 00:27:22.292
but vulnerability is not
just about fear and grief

00:27:22.292 --> 00:27:23.474
and disappointment.

00:27:23.474 --> 00:27:26.162
It's the birthplace of
everything we're hungry for:

00:27:26.162 --> 00:27:28.525
- [Oprah] Creativity.
- Joy, creativity, faith,

00:27:28.525 --> 00:27:29.731
love.
- Innovation.

00:27:29.731 --> 00:27:31.114
And all of that, yes.

00:27:31.114 --> 00:27:33.637
- And the whole thing is
there is no innovation

00:27:33.637 --> 00:27:36.822
and creativity without failure.

00:27:36.822 --> 00:27:38.440
Period.

00:27:38.440 --> 00:27:41.801
- So we got to be open enough
to take the risk to fail.

00:27:41.801 --> 00:27:42.634
- Yeah.

00:27:42.634 --> 00:27:44.208
- What I have found in
my life and what I found

00:27:44.208 --> 00:27:45.875
in my research which

00:27:46.922 --> 00:27:49.390
fueled what I did in my life

00:27:49.390 --> 00:27:52.412
is that the people who have the most

00:27:52.412 --> 00:27:53.929
courage who are willing to show them

00:27:53.929 --> 00:27:55.401
be the most vulnerable

00:27:55.401 --> 00:27:58.943
are the ones who are very clear
about who the critics are.

00:27:58.943 --> 00:28:01.098
The ones who reserve
seats for them and say,

00:28:01.098 --> 00:28:02.306
I hear you, I get it, I know

00:28:02.306 --> 00:28:04.332
where the messaging is coming from.

00:28:04.332 --> 00:28:07.968
I'm not I'm not an I'm
not buying it anymore.

00:28:07.968 --> 00:28:09.635
So to get very clear

00:28:10.659 --> 00:28:14.822
the last thing which I think
is the hardest is this,

00:28:14.822 --> 00:28:16.530
one of these seats

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:18.947
needs to be reserved for you.

00:28:20.499 --> 00:28:25.147
One of these seats needs
to be reserved for me.

00:28:25.147 --> 00:28:26.980
I need when we look up

00:28:28.434 --> 00:28:30.517
and we're putting an idea

00:28:31.723 --> 00:28:34.723
our piece of art, our design forward

00:28:36.202 --> 00:28:38.177
who do you think the biggest critic in the

00:28:38.177 --> 00:28:40.485
arena normally is?

00:28:40.485 --> 00:28:41.318
Yourself.

00:28:43.547 --> 00:28:45.214
And so definitely me

00:28:46.655 --> 00:28:48.738
like I have never watched

00:28:50.924 --> 00:28:53.646
either of those TED Talks

00:28:53.646 --> 00:28:55.673
because it's not in
service of the work for me.

00:28:55.673 --> 00:28:56.819
And I try to do things are only

00:28:56.819 --> 00:28:57.980
in service of my work

00:28:57.980 --> 00:28:59.864
because what would what would
it serve for me to watch it.

00:28:59.864 --> 00:29:01.118
I would sit there and go, oh my God

00:29:01.118 --> 00:29:02.134
suck in your stomach.

00:29:02.134 --> 00:29:04.211
Oh my God that's not what
you were going to say.

00:29:04.211 --> 00:29:06.878
You know we're so self-critical.

00:29:09.151 --> 00:29:12.476
And one of the things that I think happens

00:29:12.476 --> 00:29:14.009
and I think this happens a lot

00:29:14.009 --> 00:29:15.526
it happens in different professions

00:29:15.526 --> 00:29:18.883
but I think I see it a lot with creatives

00:29:18.883 --> 00:29:22.961
is there is an ideal of
what you're supposed to be.

00:29:22.961 --> 00:29:26.190
And what a lot of us end
up doing is we orphan

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:27.850
the parts ourselves
that don't fit what that

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:29.850
ideal is supposed to be.

00:29:31.429 --> 00:29:35.323
And what it leaves, when we
orphan all those parts of us

00:29:35.323 --> 00:29:37.258
is it just leaves the critic.

00:29:37.258 --> 00:29:40.341
And so reserved in this seat is this.

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:43.883
Where we came from.

00:29:46.119 --> 00:29:47.369
How we started.

00:29:48.804 --> 00:29:52.637
Our families, that's me
the oldest, of course.

00:29:53.958 --> 00:29:56.208
(laughing)

00:29:57.679 --> 00:29:59.718
The lost years.

00:29:59.718 --> 00:30:01.014
(laughing)

00:30:01.014 --> 00:30:04.514
The years where I was so lost and confused

00:30:05.854 --> 00:30:07.944
and hurt and disillusioned

00:30:07.944 --> 00:30:10.705
that I thought the only path to freedom

00:30:10.705 --> 00:30:12.293
was a Flock of Seagulls haircut.

00:30:12.293 --> 00:30:14.543
(laughing)

00:30:18.646 --> 00:30:21.798
The higher the hair the
closer to God we say in Texas.

00:30:21.798 --> 00:30:24.261
(laughing)

00:30:24.261 --> 00:30:26.757
The people who love us,
the moments that make us

00:30:26.757 --> 00:30:27.674
who we are.

00:30:29.462 --> 00:30:32.795
And in that chair should be this person.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:36.822
The person who believes
in what we're doing

00:30:36.822 --> 00:30:38.080
and why we're doing it.

00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:40.080
And the person who says,

00:30:41.941 --> 00:30:44.326
yeah it's so scary to show up.

00:30:44.326 --> 00:30:46.392
It feels dangerous to be seen.

00:30:46.392 --> 00:30:48.451
It's terrifying.

00:30:48.451 --> 00:30:50.944
But it is not as scary,
dangerous, or terrifying

00:30:50.944 --> 00:30:52.990
as getting to the end of our lives

00:30:52.990 --> 00:30:56.567
and thinking, what if
I would have shown up?

00:30:56.567 --> 00:30:59.150
What would have been different?

00:31:00.793 --> 00:31:02.463
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:31:02.463 --> 00:31:04.407
I hope you enjoyed, cast your vote on

00:31:04.407 --> 00:31:06.925
who you want to see in the next top 10.

00:31:06.925 --> 00:31:08.528
Check out the link in the description

00:31:08.528 --> 00:31:10.950
and go vote for the
person that you want me to

00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:12.206
profile next.

00:31:12.206 --> 00:31:14.325
I'd also like to know
which Clips resonates

00:31:14.325 --> 00:31:15.437
the most with you.

00:31:15.437 --> 00:31:17.569
What you keep from this
video and immediately

00:31:17.569 --> 00:31:20.139
apply to your life or to business somehow

00:31:20.139 --> 00:31:21.542
leave it down the comments below.

00:31:21.542 --> 00:31:23.785
I'm really curious to find
out what you have to say.

00:31:23.785 --> 00:31:26.499
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Lucille Kate Natura

00:31:26.499 --> 00:31:29.223
thank you so much Lucille for
picking up a copy of my book

00:31:29.223 --> 00:31:32.018
Your One Word and posting
the picture on Instagram.

00:31:32.018 --> 00:31:33.907
I really really really
appreciate your support

00:31:33.907 --> 00:31:36.125
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed them.

00:31:36.125 --> 00:31:38.194
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:31:38.194 --> 00:31:39.416
I believe in you.

00:31:39.416 --> 00:31:41.074
I hope you teach to believe in yourself

00:31:41.074 --> 00:31:43.306
and whatever your one word is.

00:31:43.306 --> 00:31:44.937
Much love

00:31:44.937 --> 00:31:46.319
I'll see you soon.

00:31:46.319 --> 00:31:47.160
(airplane flying)

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:50.160
(basketball bounce)

00:31:51.808 --> 00:31:54.597
- We numb vulnerability.

00:31:54.597 --> 00:31:56.472
When we're waiting for the call.

00:31:56.472 --> 00:31:57.969
When we're trying to think about

00:31:57.969 --> 00:31:59.497
if we're going, you know, it was funny.

00:31:59.497 --> 00:32:02.203
I guess Wednesday I sent
something out on Twitter

00:32:02.203 --> 00:32:04.489
on Twitter and on Facebook
that says, what how would you

00:32:04.489 --> 00:32:06.760
define vulnerability, what
makes you feel vulnerable?

00:32:06.760 --> 00:32:08.335
And within an hour and a half

00:32:08.335 --> 00:32:10.002
I had 150 responses.

00:32:11.265 --> 00:32:15.926
Because I wanted to know
you know what's out there.

00:32:15.926 --> 00:32:18.246
Having to ask my husband
for help because I'm sick

00:32:18.246 --> 00:32:21.047
and we're newly married.

00:32:21.047 --> 00:32:23.155
Initiating sex with my husband.

00:32:23.155 --> 00:32:25.850
Initiating sex with my wife.

00:32:25.850 --> 00:32:27.103
Being turned down.

00:32:27.103 --> 00:32:28.769
Asking someone out.

00:32:28.769 --> 00:32:30.807
Waiting for the doctor to call back.

00:32:30.807 --> 00:32:31.848
Getting laid off.

00:32:31.848 --> 00:32:33.243
Laying off people.

00:32:33.243 --> 00:32:35.638
This is the world we live in.

00:32:35.638 --> 00:32:38.723
We live in a vulnerable world.

00:32:38.723 --> 00:32:40.423
And one of the ways we deal with it is

00:32:40.423 --> 00:32:42.274
we numb vulnerability.

00:32:42.274 --> 00:32:44.944
And I think there's evidence,
and it's not the only

00:32:44.944 --> 00:32:47.676
reason this evidence exists.

00:32:47.676 --> 00:32:50.414
But I think that there
is a huge cause we are

00:32:50.414 --> 00:32:51.831
the most in debt,

00:32:53.199 --> 00:32:54.032
obese,

00:32:55.624 --> 00:32:57.283
addicted

00:32:57.283 --> 00:33:00.700
and medicated adult cohort in US history.

00:33:04.882 --> 00:33:06.465
The problem is why?

00:33:07.462 --> 00:33:10.914
The problem is and I learned
this from the research

00:33:10.914 --> 00:33:14.404
that you cannot selectively numb emotion.

00:33:14.404 --> 00:33:17.495
You can't say, here's the bad stuff.

00:33:17.495 --> 00:33:19.930
Here's vulnerability,
here's grief, here's shame,

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:21.589
here's fear, here's disappointment.

00:33:21.589 --> 00:33:23.800
I don't want to feel these.

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:25.678
I'm going to have a couple of beers

00:33:25.678 --> 00:33:26.888
and a banana nut muffin.

00:33:26.888 --> 00:33:29.213
(laughing)

00:33:29.213 --> 00:33:31.217
I don't want to feel these.

00:33:31.217 --> 00:33:33.512
And I know that I know
that's knowing laughter

00:33:33.512 --> 00:33:35.296
I hack into your lives for a living,

00:33:35.296 --> 00:33:37.958
I know that's (laughs) God.

00:33:37.958 --> 00:33:40.257
(laughing)

00:33:40.257 --> 00:33:42.907
You can't numb those hard feelings

00:33:42.907 --> 00:33:45.821
without numbing the
other effects or emotion.

00:33:45.821 --> 00:33:47.025
You cannot selectively numb.

00:33:47.025 --> 00:33:48.775
So when we numb those

00:33:49.696 --> 00:33:50.696
we numb joy.

00:33:52.359 --> 00:33:53.777
We numb gratitude.

00:33:53.777 --> 00:33:55.277
We numb happiness.

00:33:56.414 --> 00:33:58.497
And then we are miserable

00:33:59.631 --> 00:34:01.637
and we are looking for
purpose and meaning.

00:34:01.637 --> 00:34:03.306
And then we feel vulnerable so then

00:34:03.306 --> 00:34:05.228
we have a couple of beers
and a banana nut muffin.

00:34:05.228 --> 00:34:08.215
And it becomes this dangerous cycle.

00:34:08.215 --> 00:34:10.715
One of the things that I think

00:34:12.309 --> 00:34:14.229
that we need to think about is

00:34:14.229 --> 00:34:16.100
why in hell we numb.

00:34:16.100 --> 00:34:19.713
And it doesn't just have to be addiction.

00:34:19.713 --> 00:34:22.319
The other thing we do is we
make everything that's uncertain

00:34:22.319 --> 00:34:23.152
certain.

00:34:24.764 --> 00:34:27.133
Religion has gone from a belief

00:34:27.133 --> 00:34:29.794
in faith and mystery to certainty.

00:34:29.794 --> 00:34:32.544
I'm right, you're wrong, shut up.

00:34:33.458 --> 00:34:34.291
That's it.

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:37.581
Just certain.

00:34:37.581 --> 00:34:39.963
The more afraid we are
the more vulnerable we are

00:34:39.963 --> 00:34:41.950
the more afraid we are.

00:34:41.950 --> 00:34:43.763
This is what politics looks like today

00:34:43.763 --> 00:34:45.471
there's no discourse anymore.

00:34:45.471 --> 00:34:47.068
There's no conversation.

00:34:47.068 --> 00:34:49.111
There's just blame, you know what blame

00:34:49.111 --> 00:34:51.897
you know how blame is
described in the research?

00:34:51.897 --> 00:34:55.147
A way to discharge pain and discomfort.

00:34:57.873 --> 00:34:59.130
We perfect.

00:34:59.130 --> 00:35:00.789
Now let me check if there's
anyone who wants their life

00:35:00.789 --> 00:35:02.136
to look like this

00:35:02.136 --> 00:35:03.464
it would be me.

00:35:03.464 --> 00:35:05.700
But it doesn't work
because what we do is we

00:35:05.700 --> 00:35:08.758
take fat from our butts
and put it in our cheeks.

00:35:08.758 --> 00:35:11.008
(laughing)

00:35:12.011 --> 00:35:15.073
Which doesn't which
just I hope in 100 years

00:35:15.073 --> 00:35:16.469
people will look back and go, wow.

00:35:16.469 --> 00:35:18.853
(laughing)

00:35:18.853 --> 00:35:22.225
And we perfect most
dangerously our children.

00:35:22.225 --> 00:35:23.903
Let me tell you what we think about

00:35:23.903 --> 00:35:25.857
let me thank you very
quickly about children.

00:35:25.857 --> 00:35:28.695
They're hardwired for
struggle when they get here.

00:35:28.695 --> 00:35:31.428
When you hold those perfect
little babies in your hand

00:35:31.428 --> 00:35:34.137
our job is not to say,
look at them, look at her,

00:35:34.137 --> 00:35:35.700
she's perfect.

00:35:35.700 --> 00:35:37.745
My job is just to keep her perfect

00:35:37.745 --> 00:35:39.228
make sure she makes the
tennis team by fifth grade

00:35:39.228 --> 00:35:40.740
and Yale by seventh grade.

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:41.923
(laughing)

00:35:41.923 --> 00:35:43.119
Our job, that's not our job.

00:35:43.119 --> 00:35:45.336
Our job is to look and say
you know what you're imperfect

00:35:45.336 --> 00:35:47.259
and you're wired for struggle

00:35:47.259 --> 00:35:50.304
but you are worthy of love and belonging.

00:35:50.304 --> 00:35:51.951
That's our job.

00:35:51.951 --> 00:35:54.024
Show me a generation of
kids raised like that

00:35:54.024 --> 00:35:57.550
and we'll end the problems
I think that we see today.

00:35:57.550 --> 00:35:58.383
We pretend

00:36:00.822 --> 00:36:04.508
that what we do doesn't
have an effect on people.

00:36:04.508 --> 00:36:05.772
We do that in our personal lives,

00:36:05.772 --> 00:36:07.401
we do that corporate.

00:36:07.401 --> 00:36:10.747
Whether it's a bailout, an oil spill.

00:36:10.747 --> 00:36:12.124
A recall

00:36:12.124 --> 00:36:14.433
we pretend like what we're
doing doesn't have a huge impact

00:36:14.433 --> 00:36:15.766
on other people.

00:36:17.091 --> 00:36:19.482
Yeah I would say to
companies if this is not

00:36:19.482 --> 00:36:21.689
our first rodeo people.

00:36:21.689 --> 00:36:23.771
We just need you to be authentic and real

00:36:23.771 --> 00:36:24.604
and say

00:36:25.742 --> 00:36:27.056
we're sorry.

00:36:27.056 --> 00:36:28.139
We'll fix it.

00:36:31.415 --> 00:36:34.217
But there's another way and
I'll leave you with this.

00:36:34.217 --> 00:36:35.638
This is what I have found.

00:36:35.638 --> 00:36:37.950
To let ourselves be seen.

00:36:37.950 --> 00:36:39.090
Deeply seen.

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:40.423
Vulnerably seen.

00:36:42.740 --> 00:36:44.990
To love with our whole hearts

00:36:44.990 --> 00:36:47.161
even though there's no guarantee.

00:36:47.161 --> 00:36:48.961
And that's really hard I
can tell you as a parent

00:36:48.961 --> 00:36:51.628
that's excruciatingly difficult.

00:36:54.430 --> 00:36:56.816
To practice gratitude and joy

00:36:56.816 --> 00:36:58.711
in those moments of kind of terror

00:36:58.711 --> 00:37:01.315
when we're wondering,
can I love you this much?

00:37:01.315 --> 00:37:03.171
Can I believe in this as passionately?

00:37:03.171 --> 00:37:05.122
Can I be this fierce about this?

00:37:05.122 --> 00:37:07.612
Just to be able to stop and
instead of catastrophizing

00:37:07.612 --> 00:37:10.700
what might happen to say,
I'm just so grateful.

00:37:10.700 --> 00:37:14.611
Because to feel this
vulnerable means I'm alive.

00:37:14.611 --> 00:37:18.431
And the last, which I think is
probably the most important,

00:37:18.431 --> 00:37:20.462
is to believe that we're enough.

00:37:20.462 --> 00:37:23.525
Because when we work from
a place I believe that says

00:37:23.525 --> 00:37:24.442
I'm enough,

00:37:26.403 --> 00:37:30.588
then we stop screaming
and start listening.

00:37:30.588 --> 00:37:32.465
We're kinder and gentler
to the people around us

00:37:32.465 --> 00:37:35.967
and we're kinder and gentler to ourselves.

00:37:35.967 --> 00:37:37.467
That's all I have.

